# excluded.psv -- programs found in the archives and left out on purpose.
# name|category it would be under|what it is|why it is not on the disk|where it came from
# Rendered by tools/gen_catalog.py as the guide's `Not included' kind, off by
# default.  Microware's own products and the Microware system utilities left
# out under the authorship rule are deliberately NOT listed here.
add_errmsg|Editors|Builds the message file used by the forum 13 vi.|A helper of the forum 13 vi, and left out with it on the same terms.|EFFO forum disk 13, SOFTWARE/C/VI
adven2|Games|An adventure game written in Fortran.|It needs a Fortran compiler and linker that work end to end, and the RTF Fortran here only partly does.|comp.sources.games volume 11
bawk|Text tools|An awk-like pattern language.|gawk and a2p on this disk do the job.|Microware OS-9 archive, 6809 C section
bday|System & modules|Mails birthday greetings from a list kept by the administrator.|An administrator's tool that sends through sendmail from Unix system lists.|comp.sources.misc v06i099
belief|Amusements|A text filter from the silly collection.|Left out on content.|alt.sources, December 1992, silly.tar
biffa|Amusements|A text filter from the silly collection.|Left out on content.|alt.sources, December 1992, silly.tar
btree|Developer tools|Softfocus BTREE, a B-tree file-handling demonstration and test.|The forum disk lists it as public domain, but its own btree.doc says it no longer is and is sold.|EFFO forum disk 2
calendar (Minow)|Time & calendar|Martin Minow's calendar reminder program.|cal, calen and calender on this disk cover it.|comp.sources.unix volume 3
cbmtopbm|Graphics & images|Converts a compact bitmap to PBM; one of the early pbmexec tools.|It rejects PBM files, plain and raw, that netpbm on this disk reads and writes correctly, so it works with nothing here.|Microware OS-9 archive, GRAPHICS/pbmexec.lzh
cdungeon|Games|Dungeon, the DECUS mainframe adventure that preceded Zork, ported to C.|The game is Infocom's work, now another company's, and its notice prohibits commercial use; a question of rights this collection cannot settle.|comp.sources.games v12i068
center|Text tools|Centres lines of text.|Its terms ask that the author be contacted for redistribution rights or inclusion in a package.|comp.sources.misc v27i070
cent|Games|Centipede for terminals.|The posting carries a random-number file under a bare copyright with no grant.|comp.sources.games v01i071
checksoa|Communications|Checks a domain's SOA records across its name servers.|It is example code from a published book with no grant to redistribute it. nslookup and nsquery from the same port ship.|Microware OS-9 archive, OSK_NETWORK_ISP/bind.4.8.3.lzh
chop|Text tools|Cuts fields and columns out of lines.|cut, colrm and field on this disk do the job.|comp.sources.unix v25i001
ci|Developer tools|RCS 4 check-in: records a new revision of a file.|The distribution's own READ_ME is a non-disclosure form forbidding distribution in any form without the author's written permission. RCS 5 and later are GNU-licensed.|Microware OS-9 archive, CMDS/rcs4.lha
cmake|Compilers & build|Carl Kreider's crude make.|Its author describes it himself as crude and obsolete. make and gmake are on this disk.|Microware OS-9 archive, CMDS/carlutil.lzh
colm|Text tools|Sets a list out in columns.|column on this disk does the job.|comp.sources.unix v16i087
CommonTeX|Text tools|Pat Monardo's CommonTeX, TeX in C, as source.|It is not the source of the TeX on this disk, and a second TeX adds nothing.|Microware OS-9 archive, osk_ctexsrc.ar
co|Developer tools|RCS 4 check-out: retrieves a revision of a file.|The distribution's own READ_ME is a non-disclosure form forbidding distribution in any form without the author's written permission. RCS 5 and later are GNU-licensed.|Microware OS-9 archive, CMDS/rcs4.lha
dearc|Archives & compression|Extracts an MS-DOS .ARC archive.|It cannot read a crunched member, which nearly every real archive holds. arc on this disk reads them all.|Microware OS-9 archive, CMDS/carlutil.lzh
dg|Amusements|An entry in the 1990 International Obfuscated C Code Contest.|It depends on the preprocessor expanding the name of a directive, which standard C does not do, and neither preprocessor here builds it.|EFFO forum disk 16, SOFTWARE/C/7TH_C_CONTEST
dialog|Shells|Draws dialog boxes for shell scripts.|It draws with curses line-drawing characters and colour, which no curses library here provides.|comp.sources.misc v41i109
dinkum2|Games|Dinkum, an Australian text adventure.|Its author's rule allows no modified versions to be released, and an OS-9 build is one.|comp.sources.games v15i036
diph|Games|The game of dining philosophers.|It is built on sockets, fork and select, which this C library does not have.|comp.sources.games v13i026
dots2|Games|A visual dots-and-crosses game.|It is built on sockets, fork and select, which this C library does not have.|comp.sources.games v02i051
dsw|System & modules|A utility from the OS-9 International magazine disk.|All rights reserved by its publisher and author, with the forum's personal-use-only condition.|OS-9 International code disk, via EFFO
dumpinit|System & modules|Lists the settings of the init module.|It will not compile: six of the init module fields it prints do not exist in this SDK's header.|Microware OS-9 archive, file 2240
dynacon|Encoding & conversion|Converts CoCo Dynacalc spreadsheet files.|It came with a 6809 makefile only and is Color Computer business.|comp.os.os9, June 1989
errno|Developer tools|Explains an error number.|perr on this disk does the job.|TOP release 2 (The OS-9 Project, Munich), top.tar.Z
expreserve|Editors|Saves the buffer of the forum 13 vi when the editor dies.|A helper of the forum 13 vi, and left out with it on the same terms.|EFFO forum disk 13, SOFTWARE/C/VI
exrecover|Editors|Restores a buffer that expreserve saved for the forum 13 vi.|A helper of the forum 13 vi, and left out with it on the same terms.|EFFO forum disk 13, SOFTWARE/C/VI
e|Editors|A small fixed VT100 build of the SEDT screen editor.|Its terms make it available to customers and for internal use on condition that no modifications are made, and an OS-9 build is a modified copy.|EFFO forum disk 11, SOFTWARE/C/SEDT_EDITOR
ffix|Text tools|Expands tabs and turns other control characters into spaces.|detab, expand, pep and unp on this disk do the job; its in-place rewrite needs a module this disk does not have.|OS-9 Public Domain Utilities image, FFIX
flicker|Screen toys|An ANSI terminal teaser that inserts and deletes lines for ever.|It runs until interrupted and does not restore the terminal afterwards.|comp.sources.games v05i060
funky|Amusements|A text filter from the silly collection.|Left out on content.|alt.sources, December 1992, silly.tar
gnuplot_x11|Graphics & images|The X11 output driver for gnuplot 3.2.|It draws only on an X11 display, and its archive holds no gnuplot binary for it to serve.|Microware OS-9 archive, GRAPHICS/gnuplot32x.tar.Z
greed|Games|A field of digits: move to eat that many digits in a direction, until no move is left.|Its author asked that it not be redistributed.|Usenet posting, 1989; v_misc.ar on the hc disk
halign|Text tools|Aligns columns of text.|column on this disk does the job.|comp.sources.unix v06i016
hd|Files & directories|A hex dump.|dump, hdump and xd on this disk do the job.|TOP release 2 (The OS-9 Project, Munich), top.tar.Z
